course: money
lesson: 37
title: Trade, tariffs, rich and poor countries
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Trade is countries buying and selling things to each other, and it is why your banana says "Ecuador", your T-shirt "Bangladesh" and your phone "China".
explain: Countries trade because each is good at different things: bananas need about 27°C and Britain's summer days are often around 20°C, so hot countries grow bananas while others make cars or medicines, and they swap.
explain: Picture one giant ship carrying about 20,000 containers getting stuck in a canal: it was reported that goods were held up around the world, which shows how connected trade is, and how one blocked route can push prices up.
explain: A tariff is a tax on things brought in from abroad: a 10% tariff means £1 on every £10, so a £10 toy costs £11 to bring in; some say tariffs protect jobs at home, others say they just make things dearer for shoppers.
explain: Some countries are rich and some poor for many reasons, like history, wars, education, resources and how well things are run; printing more money does not fix it, because the same amount of food with twice the money just means twice the prices.
fact: Trade is buying and selling between countries; an import is something bought from abroad, and an export is something sold abroad.
fact: To specialise is to focus on what you are best at; hot countries grow bananas, which need about 27°C, while cooler countries make other things.
fact: A tariff is a tax on imports: a 10% tariff is £1 on every £10, so it adds £1 to a £10 toy, making it £11.
fact: It was reported that the USA put tariffs on goods from other countries; some say tariffs protect jobs at home, others say they make things dearer for shoppers.
fact: Some cheap goods are made by workers with low pay and hard conditions; a £3 T-shirt may pay its maker only a few pence, and fair trade labels try to make sure workers get a fair deal.
fact: In a rich country, an average person might have about 30 times more money a year than in a very poor country; many poor countries are getting richer over time, but big gaps remain.
fact: Most goods travel by ship, and one big ship can carry about 20,000 containers; a supply chain is all the steps that bring goods to you.
fact: Some people prefer local produce to support farmers nearby, like British apples at £2 a bag instead of imported ones at £1.50; others say imports are cheaper and help poorer countries; both views have points.
fact: Globalisation is countries being connected through trade; one phone can have parts from 10 countries that travel a total of about 100,000 miles.
fact: It was reported that when one country stopped selling grain because of a war, bread prices went up; even if wheat gets much dearer, a £1 loaf might only go up to about £1.10, because wheat is only part of a loaf's cost.
fact: A shortage is not having enough of something; countries that depend on imports are hit hard when trade is cut off.
fact: Printing twice as much money with the same amount of food just makes prices about twice as high; countries get richer by producing more, through education, roads, health and peace.
fact: Productivity is how much is made per worker; investment is spending now to get more later.
exercise: Mia's banana is from Ecuador, her T-shirt from Bangladesh and her phone from China. What is this an example of? | Trade between countries | Every country making everything itself | Shops making all goods in the back room | Children travelling the world to shop
exercise: What is an import? | Something bought from another country | Something that is sold to another country | A tax that every worker pays on their wages | A shop that sells only food grown nearby
exercise: Why doesn't the UK grow bananas? | They need about 27°C and Britain is too cool | Bananas are banned in Britain by law | British soil is far too salty for any kind of fruit | Bananas only grow on islands
exercise: A £10 toy gets a 10% tariff. What does it cost to bring in? | £11 | £10.10 | £20 | £1
exercise: What is a tariff? | A tax on things brought in from abroad | A discount on things made at home | A ship that carries goods across the sea | A fee for taking money from a cash machine
exercise: Some say tariffs protect jobs at home. What do others say? | They make things more expensive for shoppers | They make everything completely free for shoppers | They have no effect on anything or anyone at all | They are paid by the children who buy imported toys
exercise: Ella's £3 T-shirt might mean what for the worker who made it? | Very low pay, maybe only a few pence | A huge wage of about £3 for each shirt | A free holiday paid by the shop | Nothing, T-shirts are made by robots only
exercise: What do fair trade labels try to do? | Make sure workers get a fair deal | Make all clothes cost exactly £3 | Stop countries trading altogether | Tell you which shop is cheapest
exercise: Why are some countries rich and others poor? | Many reasons: history, wars, education, resources and how things are run | Rich countries simply print more money than poor ones | Poor countries choose to be poor on purpose | Only the weather decides it
exercise: About how many containers can one big ship carry? | About 20,000 | About 20 | About 200 | About 2,000,000
exercise: A ship gets stuck in a canal and the news says shops might run short. Why? | Most goods travel by ship, so a blocked route delays lots of them | The ship was carrying every item in every shop | Shops all have to close whenever a ship stops anywhere in the world | Canals are where all the shops are built
exercise: British apples are £2 a bag and imported ones £1.50. What is the difference? | 50p | £3.50 | £1.50 | £1
exercise: Oscar's grandad says "Buy British" and his aunt says imports help poorer countries. What is true? | Both views have points | Only the grandad has a reason | Only the aunt has a reason | Neither is talking about trade
exercise: Kai's phone has parts from 10 countries. What is the word for the world being linked like this? | Globalisation | Inflation | Specialisation | Recession
exercise: Wheat gets much dearer because of a war. What might happen to a £1 loaf? | It might go up a bit, to about £1.10 | It would go up to exactly £2 | It would go down to 70p | It would stay at £1 for ever
exercise: Ruby asks why poor countries don't just print more money. What would happen? | Prices would rise, but there would be no more food | Everyone would be rich and nothing would change | Food would double just like the money, so it would all even out | Other countries would send them free money
exercise: How do countries usually get richer over time? | By producing more, through education, roads, health and peace | By printing as much money as they can | By stopping all trade with other countries | By making all their workers stay at home
test: Josh says, "UK doesn't grow bananas cos it's too cold, innit." His mate says, "Nah, it's cos bananas are banned." Who's right? | Josh: bananas need about 27°C, warmer than our summers | His mate: bananas are banned in the UK by a very old food law | Both: bananas are banned here because our weather is too cold | Neither: the UK actually grows most of the world's bananas
test: Kai asks, "What's a tariff?" His cousin says, "It's like a fancy word for a tiara, lol." What's the real meaning? | A tax on goods brought in from abroad | A crown that is worn by the president at trade meetings | A boat that brings goods to port | A discount on toys made at home
test: An American cousin says, "My dad's fixin' to buy a car, but with them tariffs it's gonna cost more, y'all." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) Why would it cost more? | A tariff adds tax to imported cars or parts | Tariffs make all cars free in America | Tariffs only apply to bananas and T-shirts | Cars get cheaper the more tariffs there are
test: What would happen if the UK stopped all trade with other countries? | Many things, like bananas, would vanish or cost far more | Nothing, the UK makes everything it needs | Prices would fall because there's less choice | Bananas would start growing in British gardens
test: Leo says, "One boat stuck and the shops run short? No way, that's cap." ("Cap" means a lie.) What's true? | One big ship carries about 20,000 containers, so it really matters | He's right, one ship carries just a few boxes | Shops never get goods from ships | Ships only carry tourists, not goods
test: Ella feels bad about her £3 T-shirt. Her mate says, "Cheap clothes are just made by robots, so who cares." What's more accurate? | Some cheap goods are made by low-paid workers in hard conditions | All cheap clothes are made by robots in big factories with no people at all | Workers who make cheap clothes are paid the most | Cheap clothes are made by volunteers for fun
test: Oscar's grandad, from Somerset, says, "Buy British, my lover, support our farmers." ("My lover" is a friendly West Country word, like "dear".) His aunt says, "Imports are cheaper and help poorer countries." What's the fair summary? | Both views have points, and people choose differently | The grandad is right, and all imports should be banned now | The aunt is right, and farmers here don't matter at all | Neither, because apples are never brought in from abroad
test: In a cartoon, a poor country becomes rich in one day by printing trucks of money. What would really happen? | Prices would shoot up, and people would be no richer | Everyone would be rich for ever with no problems | Other countries would copy and all be rich | The money would turn into gold overnight
test: Kai's dad says, "Bloody hell, a world tour in my pocket!" about his phone with parts from 10 countries. If each part travelled 10,000 miles, what's the total? | 100,000 miles | 10,000 miles | 1,000 miles | 1,000,000 miles
test: The toy is £20 and the tariff is 10% — no, it went up to 25%. What does it cost to bring in now? | £25 | £22 | £45 | £20.25
test: A grain-selling country stops selling because of a war, as was reported. Which country is hit hardest? | One that depends on buying grain from abroad | One that grows all its own grain on its own farms at home | One that doesn't eat bread at all | One that only sells cars
exam: It is given that an article valued at £10 is subject to an import tariff of ten per cent. Determine the cost of importing the article. | £11 | £10.10 | £20 | £1
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a tariff? | A tax levied upon goods imported from abroad | A reduction in the price of goods produced at home | A vessel engaged in the carriage of goods by sea | A charge levied upon the withdrawal of cash
exam: Identify the principal consequence of doubling the quantity of money in circulation whilst the quantity of goods remains unaltered. | An approximate doubling of prices without any increase in prosperity | A doubling of the prosperity of every inhabitant | A halving of the prices of all goods | No consequence whatsoever for prices or prosperity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the debate between the purchase of local produce and of imports. | Each position has merit, and opinion is divided | Local produce is in every respect superior, and all agree | Imports are in every respect superior, and all agree | Imports are prohibited by law in every country
